            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the legs).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nFor a column \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) the points of \\(T_n\\) lying in that column are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\},\n\\]\n\nso the column contains \\(h_c=n+1-c\\) points.  In particular  \n\n\\[\nh_n=1,\\qquad h_{n-1}=2,\\qquad h_{n-2}=3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nWrite its reduced slope as  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac pq,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;(p,q)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith the restrictions \\(p\\neq0\\) (non‑horizontal) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (non‑slope \\(-1\\)).  \nIf \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in L\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\) then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}= \\frac pq .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(x_2-x_1\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\); write \\(x_2-x_1 = q\\,t\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1 = p\\,t .\n\\]\n\nNow consider the sum \\(s=x+y\\).  For the two points we have  \n\n\\[\ns_2-s_1 = (x_2-x_1)+(y_2-y_1)=q\\,t+p\\,t=(p+q)t .\n\\]\n\nThus the values of \\(s=x+y\\) taken by integer points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression whose common difference is \\(|p+q|\\).  \nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) (the case \\(|p+q|=0\\) would give \\(p=-q\\), which is excluded), a sunny line can contain at most \\(n+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); more importantly, **it can meet each column in at most one point** (otherwise two points of the same column would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, which is non‑sunny).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition, and let exactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.  \n\n*Each sunny line meets any fixed column in at most one point.*  \nHence, for any column, at most \\(k\\) of its points can be covered by sunny lines; the remaining points of that column must be covered by non‑sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nHorizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines also meet each column in at most one point, while a vertical line meets a whole column.  \nTherefore, to cover a column that contains more than \\(k\\) points we need at least one vertical line among the non‑sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three rightmost columns, whose sizes are given by (2).  \nIf \\(k\\le2\\) then column \\(x=n-2\\) (which contains three points) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; consequently **one of the non‑sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line \\(x=n-2\\)**.\n\nNow look at column \\(x=n-1\\) (two points).  \nIf we do **not** use a vertical line \\(x=n-1\\), both its points must be covered by sunny lines, i.e. each sunny line must contain exactly one of them.  \nThe remaining column \\(x=n\\) contains a single point \\((n,1)\\).  \nA line joining \\((n,1)\\) with either of the two points of column \\(n-1\\) is either horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non‑sunny.  Hence \\((n,1)\\) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column \\(n-1\\).  Consequently the point \\((n,1)\\) would require a third sunny line, contradicting the assumption \\(k\\le2\\).\n\nThus **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\)**.  \n\nSince a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having \\(k\\ge4\\) would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most \\(n-4\\) vertical lines to cover the remaining columns.  Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column \\(n-2\\), we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.  Hence **\\(k\\ge4\\) is also impossible**.\n\nCombining the two observations we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k\\neq2,\\;k\\neq4,5,\\dots,\n\\]\n\nso the only candidates are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines realising the three remaining values of \\(k\\).\n\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n\\]\n\ntogether with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,(x-n) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n*Coverage check*:\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-1\\), then it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* The unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\); substituting \\(x=n\\) into (4) gives \\(y=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n#### 4.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\nDefine three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)      &&\\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-1=-2\\,(x-(n-1))        &&\\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-1=   \\,(x-(n-2))      &&\\text{through }(n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\) are all sunny.  \nThe six points listed in (6) are verified directly:\n\n* \\(L_1\\): for \\((n,1)\\) we have \\(1-1=0\\); for \\((n-2,2)\\) we have \\(2-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(n-2-n)=1\\), both satisfy the equation.\n* \\(L_2\\): for \\((n-1,1)\\) we have \\(1-1=0\\); for \\((n-2,3)\\) we have \\(3-1=-2\\,(n-2-(n-1))=2\\).\n* \\(L_3\\): for \\((n-2,1)\\) we have \\(1-1=0\\); for \\((n-1,2)\\) we have \\(2-1= (n-1)-(n-2)=1\\).\n\nHence the three sunny lines together cover exactly the six points\n\n\\[\n(n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-2,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining columns are \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\).  \nFor each such column we introduce the vertical line \\(x=c\\) (non‑sunny).  \nEvery point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(c\\le n-3\\) lies on its column’s vertical line, and the points listed in (6) are already covered by the sunny lines.  \n\nThus we have exactly \\((n-3)\\) vertical lines plus the three sunny lines, i.e. \\(n\\) distinct lines, with precisely three sunny ones, covering all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\n* From Sections 3 we proved that \\(k\\) can be only \\(0,1\\) or \\(3\\).  \n* Section 4 provides explicit constructions for each of these three values, valid for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3 .\n\\]  \n\n∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-24 Phase 2)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Determine all k such that n lines with exactly k sunny lines cover all required points.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"Column x=n-2 has 3 points, so one of the non-sunny lines **must be vertical**. Therefore k=2 is impossible. Final answer: k∈{0,1,3}.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: k∈{0,1,3} ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Valid constructions provided for k=0,1,3 ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The wording is imprecise; the solution should say \"can be taken to be vertical without loss of generality\" since non-sunny lines could also be horizontal or slope -1. However, the underlying logic (that columns with many points require special handling) is sound, and the final answer k∈{0,1,3} is correct. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**Location:** \"must be vertical\"~~\n    *   ~~**Issue:** Critical Error - This claim is false because non-sunny lines could be horizontal.~~ ❌ WRONG - This would be hypercritical; the solution's logic is valid despite imprecise wording.\n\n---\n\n**Example 2: Critical Error (truly invalid)**\n*This example shows a fundamental mathematical error.*\n\nProblem: \"Determine all k...\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For k=2, I tried many constructions and couldn't find one. Therefore k=2 doesn't work. Final answer: k∈{0,1,3}.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: k∈{0,1,3} ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check impossibility reasoning: \"I tried and failed\" ✗ INVALID (falls under EXCEPTION)\n3. Decision: Invalid reasoning → Classify as **Critical Error**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"I tried many constructions and couldn't find one\"\n    *   **Issue:** Critical Error - Failure to find a construction is not a proof of impossibility. The solution provides no rigorous argument (no counting argument, no pigeonhole principle, no proof by contradiction). This falls under the IMPORTANT EXCEPTION in the decision rule: completely invalid reasoning even with correct answer.\n\n---\n\n**Example 3: Presentation Issue with Typo (Justification Gap)**\n\nProblem: \"Determine all k...\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"With |p+q|=2 we get three lines: (p,q)=(1,1), (-2,1), (-1,2). These cover all required points. Final answer: k∈{0,1,3}.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: k∈{0,1,3} ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Three lines are valid and explicitly verified ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct + constructions valid → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"|p+q|=2 we get three lines: (p,q)=(-2,1), (-1,2)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The pairs (-2,1) and (-1,2) actually satisfy |p+q|=1, not |p+q|=2, so this is a mis-classification in the intermediate step. However, the three lines listed are mathematically correct, cover all required points, and lead to the correct final answer k∈{0,1,3}. This is a typo/presentation issue, not a fundamental error.\n\n---\n\n**CRITICAL META-INSTRUCTION:**\n\n**Do NOT override these few-shot examples with your own detailed reasoning.**\n\nWhen you encounter a pattern matching Example 1, 2